  (RUNTIME-EXCEPTION.md).
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  sqlite.pcpp - the SQLite unit: `import sqlite;` uses SQLite (an embedded SQL
  database engine: a whole database in one file, or in memory) from
  PaskalCPP. A self-describing vendor module: it owns every include path,
  library path and link library it needs, per official target. The library is
  libsqlite3.a (one per target), built by PaskalCPP-Libs' build-sqlite.cmd from
  the official amalgamation release, linked by @linklibrary: every module of
  the program shares the one copy, and a program links only the libraries it
  imports.

  The API is SQLite's C API as it ships (https://sqlite.org/c3ref/intro.html),
  called straight from PaskalCPP: sqlite3_open / sqlite3_open_v2 give a
  `ptr to sqlite3` (":memory:" = a private database in memory),
  sqlite3_prepare_v2 compiles one statement into a `ptr to sqlite3_stmt`,
  sqlite3_bind_* fill its ? parameters (from 1), sqlite3_step runs it
  (SQLITE_ROW per result row, SQLITE_DONE at the end), sqlite3_column_* read a
  row (from 0), sqlite3_finalize frees the statement and sqlite3_close the
  database. sqlite3_exec runs SQL text with no results to read. A call that
  fails returns its result code; sqlite3_errmsg(db) is the reason in UTF-8.
  Text crosses as UTF-8: a PaskalCPP string's .c_str() goes in, and
  sqlite3_column_text gives `ptr to const uint8`, read as text through
  `ptr to const char(...)`. A string bound with SQLITE_TRANSIENT is copied by
  SQLite, so the string may change before the statement runs.

  Built with: SQLITE_THREADSAFE=1 (serialized: a connection may be used from
  several threads), FTS5 (full-text search), R*Tree indexes and the SQL math
  functions; JSON is built in.
==============================================================================*/

module unit sqlite;

@addincludepath "$P:res/libs/vendor/sqlite/include";

// SQLite needs the C runtime: on Windows mingw's CRT and kernel32 (threads,
// file locking; both linked by default), on Linux libc, libm (the math
// functions), libpthread (its mutexes) and libdl (sqlite3_load_extension)
@ifdef TARGET_X86_64_WINDOWS_GNU
  @addlibrarypath "$P:res/libs/vendor/sqlite/lib/x86_64-windows-gnu";
@elseif TARGET_X86_64_LINUX_GNU
  @addlibrarypath "$P:res/libs/vendor/sqlite/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu";
  @linklibrary "m";
  @linklibrary "pthread";
  @linklibrary "dl";
@else
  @message error "sqlite: unsupported target";
@endif

@linklibrary "sqlite3";

// The header goes to this unit's header, so every importer sees SQLite's API
cpp header;

#include <sqlite3.h>

end.
